<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orange River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gariep River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orange River is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, flowing about 2,200 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a> highlands west to the Atlantic, where it forms the border between South Africa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>. In the 18th and 19th centuries the lands north of it were home to Nama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korana">Korana</a> communities and became a frontier of mission stations, trekkers and colonial expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orange-river.jpg" alt="Orange River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: paffy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OrangeRiverUpington.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Orange rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> mountains on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>, at an altitude of over 3,000 m; in Lesotho its upper course is known as the Senqu. It flows westward through South Africa, passing into the Gariep Dam and later the Vanderkloof Dam, and meets its main tributary, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal River</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>. It then crosses the arid southern Kalahari and Namaqualand, reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> at 20°E, forms the border for 550 km and enters the Atlantic between Oranjemund and Alexander Bay.</p><p>Its catchment, including the Vaal, covers 973,000 km2, equal to about 77% of the land area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, although 38% of it lies outside the country. Rainfall is about 2,000 mm a year at the source but under 50 mm at the mouth. In the lower river the Augrabies Falls drop the water 56 m over a course of 18 km.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Some of the earliest precolonial inhabitants called the river ǂNūǃarib, referring to its black colour, or Kai!Arib, &quot;Great River&quot;, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> name Gariep derives. The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> name was a translation, Groote Rivier. Colonel Robert Gordon, commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC) garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, named it the Orange River on a trip to the interior in 1779, in honour of William V of Orange. A popular belief that the name describes the colour of the water is incorrect. Its tributary the Vaal takes its name from ǀHaiǃarib, &quot;pale river&quot;.</p><p>In 1867 the first diamond found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, the Eureka Diamond, was picked up near Hopetown on the Orange. Two years later a much larger stone, the Star of South Africa, was found in the same area and set off a diamond rush. This was soon eclipsed by the rush to mine kimberlite directly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> in 1871, although alluvial diamonds continued to be found in the river. Hippopotami, once abundant there, were hunted to extermination in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river supplies water for irrigation and hydroelectric power. The Orange River Project, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a>'s government during the apartheid era, centres on the Gariep Dam near Colesberg: from there water flows west to the Vanderkloof Dam and south through the Orange-Fish Tunnel to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>. The Vanderkloof power station was the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> built entirely underground. The Lesotho Highlands Water Project delivers water to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal River</a> system. Its mouth is a Ramsar site, and since the end of apartheid the name Gariep has found greater favour in official South African correspondence, while Orange has greater international recognition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orange River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The discovery of diamonds near the <b>Orange River</b> in 1867 and at Kimberley in 1871 transformed the economy, financing railways northwards and drawing migrant labour from across southern Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1848 the Cape governor Sir Harry Smith proclaimed British sovereignty over the region as the <b>Orange River</b> Sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Britain annexed Natal in 1843 and proclaimed the <b>Orange River</b> Sovereignty in 1848, many trekkers moved north of the Vaal River and settled in scattered communities around Potchefstroom, Lydenburg, Zoutpansberg and Utrecht.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sadr finds no evidence of widespread Khoekhoe settlements or large herds south of the <b>Orange River</b> before 1000 CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bondelswarts, in Nama !Gami-ǂnun, were a Khoekhoe-speaking Nama group living north of the <b>Orange River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bondelswarts">Bondelswarts</a></li><li><blockquote>Griqua, people of mixed Khoekhoe and settler descent from the Cape, had gathered at the London Missionary Society station of Klaarwater north of the <b>Orange River</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/griqualand-west">Griqualand West</a></li></ul></section>
